Sealed Interface

Definition : 
  -> Introduced in Java 17.
  -> A sealed interface restricts which classes or interfaces can implement or extend it.
  -> Only explicitly permitted classes/interfaces are allowed.

Syntax:
public sealed interface Payment
        permits UPI, CreditCard, Cash {
}


Why Sealed Interfaces?
Normally, any class can implement an interface.

interface Payment {}
class UPI implements Payment {}
class CreditCard implements Payment {}
class Crypto implements Payment {}   // Allowed

Suppose only UPI, CreditCard, and Cash should be valid payment methods.
A normal interface cannot restrict implementations.

Solution: Use a sealed interface.

public sealed interface Payment
        permits UPI, CreditCard, Cash {
}

Now only these classes can implement it.
final class UPI implements Payment {}
final class CreditCard implements Payment {}
final class Cash implements Payment {}

Attempting:
class Crypto implements Payment {}

Compilation Error: Class Crypto is not allowed in the sealed hierarchy.


Rules:

Rule 1) : Every permitted implementation must declare one modifier:
        -> final
        -> sealed
        -> non-sealed

Example
final class Cash implements Payment {}

Rule 2 ) Every permitted class must explicitly implement the interface.

Incorrect:
sealed interface Payment
        permits Cash {}

final class Cash {
}

Compilation Error

Correct:

final class Cash implements Payment {}

Rule 3) Permitted classes/interfaces must belong to the same package/module as the sealed interface.

Example

payment
    Payment

payment2
    UPI

Compilation Error



Three Possible Modifiers

1. final
Stops further inheritance.

final class Cash implements Payment {}

Cannot extend Cash.


2. sealed

Continue controlled hierarchy.

sealed class CreditCard
        implements Payment
        permits VisaCard, MasterCard {
}
final class VisaCard extends CreditCard {}

final class MasterCard extends CreditCard {}


3. non-sealed: Removes restrictions.

non-sealed class UPI
        implements Payment {
}


Now anyone can extend UPI.

class GooglePay extends UPI {}
class PhonePe extends UPI {}
class Paytm extends UPI {}


